<p>The U.S. Justice Department is preparing to seek an indictment against the 94-year-old former Cuban President Raúl Castro, according to people familiar with the matter and a Justice Department official cited by AP, Reuters and CBS News. A grand jury would have to approve any indictment, and several accounts said prosecutors were examining Castro’s alleged role in Cuba’s 1996 shootdown of aircraft operated by the Miami exile group Brothers to the Rescue. The case centers on the Feb. 24, 1996, downing of two small civilian planes by Cuban Air Force MiG jets, an incident that killed four Cuban Americans. Castro was Cuba’s defense minister at the time. He later succeeded his brother Fidel Castro as president and formally left the Communist Party leadership in 2021, though several accounts describe him as still influential in Cuban politics. The Trump administration has increased pressure on Havana, with AP citing threats of possible military action and other reports noting stepped-up surveillance flights, a CIA director visit to Cuba, and an energy crisis marked by severe fuel shortages and blackouts. President Trump was asked about the possibility of a Castro indictment while returning from China on Friday and directed questions to the Justice Department, according to Noticias SIN; U.S. authorities had not publicly announced charges as of May 15.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/7OkJmDIseRmGaswAeSHHNuII-vN520Z2QE_WmKWjirPMBR-oHb_s1klqC3k9NdrfZ2Ql_rQwfOqQqxWfUc-PtN9VeLEWldvfZ_jTvmJ1MauIq2INaKqhnPqQ9JNXaHKT2LlhhPxq5LGe2CWDWl1A2QNe9oOJPdI6ZwlNldWhCApsU85wsGkkx-tc5MInBogncoII__NZXBQ6JnSJ7rh1n7_i8O3yS01r4ziYt7SMei2YWFgagqxh9uXgn11pTf4SuYweIaVyIRGGDnHS6g' alt='Cuba’s former president Raul Castro attending a May Day parade in Havana.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Expected timing: A Justice Department official told Reuters the Trump administration planned to unveil criminal charges next Wednesday, May 20.</li><li>Miami venue: The Miami Herald, cited by Spanish-language outlets, said any announcement could take place at Miami’s Freedom Tower during a Cuban Independence Day event honoring the victims.</li><li>Earlier review: NBC News had earlier reported that the Trump administration had begun examining whether federal prosecutors could charge members of Cuba’s regime or Communist Party with crimes.</li><li>Aid offer: Secretary of State Marco Rubio renewed an offer of €85 million in aid on the condition that the Catholic Church, rather than Cuba’s government, distribute it, Euronews reported.</li></ul><blockquote>Adelante, ya era hora - Florida Gov. Ron DeSantis</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Federal prosecutors: CNN reported that prosecutors were working to secure charges while the scope of the investigation remained unclear; it said they had reviewed several possible counts, including some tied to the Cuban military. (<a href='https://krdo.com/news/2026/05/15/us-justice-department-working-to-indict-former-cuban-president-raul-castro/'>CNN via KRDO</a>)</li><li>Cuban government: Cuba said CIA Director John Ratcliffe met in Havana with Interior Minister Lázaro Alberto Álvarez Casas and other Cuban officials, and described the encounter as part of contacts around Washington’s reform demands and humanitarian relief. (<a href='https://krdo.com/news/2026/05/15/cuba-says-cia-director-john-ratcliffe-met-with-interior-minister-in-havana/'>CNN via KRDO</a>)</li><li>South Florida Republican lawmakers: Newsis reported that the charging effort followed calls from Republican lawmakers in South Florida for an investigation into the 1996 case. (<a href='https://www.newsis.com/view/NISX20260516_0003632141'>Newsis</a>)</li><li>Richard Feinberg, UC San Diego emeritus professor: Newsis reported that he said indicting Castro could appeal to South Florida voters but that persuading Americans to support a new war would be difficult. (<a href='https://www.newsis.com/view/NISX20260516_0003632141'>Newsis</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><